WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.112
Every line starts somewhere.

00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:03.152
Let's find where.

00:00:03.702 --> 00:00:04.713
Here's the stage.

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:07.152
The performer is walking toward the mark.

00:00:07.702 --> 00:00:14.455
On this graph, x is the time in seconds, and y is how far the performer still has to go.

00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:19.844
When the clock first starts, no time has passed, so x is zero.

00:00:20.394 --> 00:00:23.635
And x equals zero is right on the y-axis.

00:00:24.185 --> 00:00:27.100
Now look where the line crosses that axis.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:27.933
It's at six.

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:30.206
That's the point zero, six.

00:00:30.206 --> 00:00:31.929
We call it the y-intercept.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:33.978
It's the starting value.

00:00:33.978 --> 00:00:37.666
At zero seconds, the performer is six meters from the mark.

00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:40.041
The pool works the same way.

00:00:40.041 --> 00:00:46.689
The swimmer glides three meters before the clock starts, so that line begins at the point zero, three.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:51.168
So here's the trick: go to x equals zero, and read the y-value.

00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:52.665
That's your y-intercept.
